\section{Derived / suffixed columns (not raw schema fields, but load-bearing)}{
The schema above describes what an adapter must produce. The generic
pipeline then derives further columns from it using a consistent suffix
convention — documented here because P3-06 in particular introduces the
first of these (\code{.valid}) at the adapter boundary rather than deeper in
the pipeline:
\itemize{
\item \strong{\verb{<col>.valid}} — raw column value, \code{NA}-masked at invalid/
below-threshold samples; raw \verb{<col>} itself is left byte-identical.
Produced by adapter \code{normalize_validity()}. Applies to \code{gazeLeftX/Y},
\code{gazeRightX/Y}, \code{pupilLeft/Right}, \code{distanceLeftZ/RightZ}.
\item \strong{\verb{<col>.int}} — interpolated (gap-filled) version of a \code{.valid}
column. Produced by \code{interpolateGaze()}.
\item \strong{\verb{<col>.eyeSelect}} — per-eye columns collapsed to a single
\code{gazeX}/\code{gazeY}/\code{distanceZ}/\code{pupil} series per the chosen
\code{eyeSelection_method}. Produced by \code{eyeSelection()}.
\item \strong{\verb{<col>.smooth}} — smoothed (denoised) version. Produced by
\code{smoothGaze()}/\code{smoothVelocity()}.
\item \strong{\verb{<col>_va}} — visual-angle-converted gaze coordinate (degrees).
Produced by \code{calculateGaze_va()}.
\item \strong{\verb{<col>.preprocessed_px} / \verb{_mm} / \verb{_va} / \verb{_va_ms}} — final output
columns. Produced by \code{assignFinalColumnNames()}.
}
}

\section{\code{geometry_type} and the schema}{
Every adapter declares \code{geometry_type} (\code{"screen"} | \code{"headmounted"} |
\code{"none"}, see \code{R/adapter-interface.R}, P3-02). All Tobii adapters as of
Phase 3 are \code{"screen"}; the schema above (\code{px} gaze units, \code{mm}
distances, screen-relative visual angle) reflects that. \code{geometry_type}
is what will gate whether pixel/visual-angle conversion and distance-
based logic run at all for a future \code{"headmounted"} adapter — the schema
itself does not change shape, but the unit/semantics of \code{gazeLeftX/Y}
and \code{distanceLeftZ/RightZ} for a non-screen adapter are an explicitly
open question (Phase 4/P4-06), not resolved here.
}

